 and has a  mission to sort out this  cursed business and while there are many  different contextual reasons for this  setup it does mirror yuji's beginnings  quite a bit  which i do like looking at it in  retrospect at this stage after  experiencing yuji's journey whereas it  may have felt a bit repetitive if i'd  started with zero and then went straight  into the actual series with a very  similar setup i mean maybe maybe not  that's just the feeling i get that is  the last huge comparison i'll be making  though because in regards to uta he is a  very different character almost  completely on the other end of the  spectrum being meek  weak and with very little belief in  himself whatsoever at least to start  with and that does make him quite  relatable because whilst the brave and  strong characters are fun to live  through vicariously  if most people found themselves  embroiled in this cursed world they  would probably be  far more uta-like in nature and i did  very much enjoy his story the tragedy of  rika being killed and then being  manifested as an insane curse being due  to a promise and nakatami  is very very clever with how that  information was dished out first  portraying it as if rika's feelings  caused this phenomena when in reality  it was that naughty naughty utah and i  also think this is the first time that  we've properly focused on love as a  source of negative energy rather than  the more straightforward things like oh  you bullied me or you destroyed my  environment or even you killed my mother  and then you killed me  sort of stuff but in terms of love it's  almost the opposite to the coast  equation that we export in gojo's past  with the whole negative times negative  equals positive  whereas love can be twisted in such a  way that it's positive times positive  equals negative  and i'm well aware that that doesn't fit  into mathematical sense because positive  times positive  still going to end up as a positive but  you know what love doesn'