n a very basic pose in the  prequel and this is going to sound  incredibly incredibly minor but one  thing that is really neglected with gojo  specifically  is his hands like seriously in jiu-jitsu  saying gojo's hands are like half of his  character akutami puts a ton of effort  into gojo's body language especially the  hands and that's why i believe he comes  across with such clarity because you  know  something crazy like 55 of communication  is purely visual and that is very very  important in the manga medium because we  don't have access to the remaining 45  percent of verbal communication  due to the fact that we can't really  hear the tone of the characters i guess  our imagination does kind of fill it in  but that's not something the author can  control as well as body language and  yeah i'm starting to go on a bit of a  tangent here but what i'm trying to say  is that between this prequel and  jiu-jitsu  akutami pretty seriously leveled up as  an artist and most of it has to do with  very minor little details like this  another example would be that curses in  jiu jitsu and zero tend to be far less  detailed and grotesque they very much  actually blend into the world as  presented whereas in the sequel series  these curses are often so like bizarrely  detailed that they pop out at you as  something that should not exist with  everything else around it which is great  because that's the entire idea behind  them being these unnatural aberrations  and all that with the second year  students or i guess first-year students  here they were very nice to see in a  more primary  focus but a lot of it is going over  information that we already know  because it needed to be restated in  jutsu kaisen so we have the same story  unraveling maki a showcase of inumaki  and actually panda he even has the exact  same introduction where he is just  referred to as  panda with no explanation whatsoever  just like fushigura did which  is pretty funny and do enjoy me some  panda